# translated-by: libretranslate
msgid "**Trusted-Header SSO Bridge:**"
msgstr "**Usaldusisiku SSO sild:**"

msgid "**Trusted-Header SSO Bridge:**   When the oauth2-proxy SSO flavor is active, Snipe-IT's native `loginViaRemoteUser()` is enabled via the `Setting` model (`login_remote_user_enabled`, `login_remote_user_header_name = INFINITO_SSO_USER`). nginx gates `/login` through oauth2-proxy/Keycloak, injects the verified `X-Forwarded-Preferred-Username` header and sends the `sso_proxy_secret` credential as `X-Infinito-Sso-Proxy` with every request. Apache in the container copies the username into the `INFINITO_SSO_USER` server variable only when that secret matches `INFINITO_SSO_PROXY_SECRET`. Snipe-IT matches the username against `users.username` (the user must pre-exist and be activated, see the RBAC feature below) to mint a native `snipeit_session`. Visitors without a matching activated user fall through to the normal login form, where a directory sign-in creates the account. The password/LDAP login form stays available as a fallback (`login_common_disabled = 0`), and the in-app logout is redirected to the central OIDC end-session URL."
msgstr ""

# translated-by: libretranslate
msgid "**Trusted-header SSO bridge:** Establishes the owner session from the oauth2-proxy identity (see below)."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Trusted-proxy authentication:** With single sign-on, the gateway runs in ``gateway.auth.mode: \"trusted-proxy\"``. It reads the signed-in user from the ``X-Forwarded-Preferred-Username`` header the front proxy sets from the OAuth2 proxy response, requires ``X-Forwarded-User`` and the secret header only the front proxy sends, and leaves ``gateway.auth.trustedProxy.allowUsers`` empty: the OAuth2 proxy admits only the role groups in ``sso.oauth2.allowed_groups``, so a user added to such a group in Keycloak reaches the gateway at the next sign-in. The Control UI needs no gateway token in this mode; device pairing still applies to every new browser."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Trusted-proxy authentication:** With single sign-on, the gateway runs in `gateway.auth.mode: \"trusted-proxy\"`. It reads the signed-in user from the `X-Forwarded-Preferred-Username` header the front proxy sets from the OAuth2 proxy response, requires `X-Forwarded-User` and the secret header only the front proxy sends, and leaves `gateway.auth.trustedProxy.allowUsers` empty: the OAuth2 proxy admits only the role groups in `sso.oauth2.allowed_groups`, so a user added to such a group in Keycloak reaches the gateway at the next sign-in. The Control UI needs no gateway token in this mode; device pairing still applies to every new browser."
msgstr ""

